job-control         # ^Z, jobs, bg, kill %1 and the wording of every notice
                    # match dash as of Iteration 324. What differs is WHEN the
                    # completion notice appears: this shell reaps in the notice
                    # pass, so it reports at the first prompt after the job
                    # ends, while dash reaps in its wait loop and reports one
                    # prompt later. Both are before a prompt, as POSIX asks.
                    # Which prompt that is depends on how fast the killed job
                    # exits, so on a slow machine this shell's notice lands
                    # where dash's does and the case PASSES: an ARMv7 board
                    # reported NOW-PASSES (Iteration 416). It is a race, not
                    # a regression either way; the harness reports it and
